From: Roki Buildroot <noreply@roki.local>
Subject: [PATCH] dinit: keep standard descriptors valid without a console

When dinit runs as PID 1 it tries to attach standard input/output/error to
/dev/console. With `console=null`, opening that device can fail. The remaining
closed file descriptors may then be re-used by the control socket while dinit
still registers fd 0 as its console input watcher, preventing normal boot.

Use /dev/null for the relevant descriptors when the kernel console is absent.
This keeps the appliance headless without routing a serial console to the
board MCU.

---
 src/dinit.cc | 6 ++++++
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/dinit.cc b/src/dinit.cc
index 39e3f6e..8b74a1c 100644
--- a/src/dinit.cc
+++ b/src/dinit.cc
@@ -501,10 +501,16 @@ int dinit_main(int argc, char **argv)
     if (am_system_mgr) {
         // setup STDIN, STDOUT, STDERR so that we can use them
         int onefd = open("/dev/console", O_RDONLY, 0);
+        if (onefd == -1) {
+            onefd = open("/dev/null", O_RDONLY, 0);
+        }
         if (onefd != -1) {
             dup2(onefd, 0);
         }
         int twofd = open("/dev/console", O_RDWR, 0);
+        if (twofd == -1) {
+            twofd = open("/dev/null", O_WRONLY, 0);
+        }
         if (twofd != -1) {
             dup2(twofd, 1);
             dup2(twofd, 2);
-- 
